32,42125,27229,11,18,15,1106,7633,27704,524,3433,838,3025,43539,27229,11,18,15,1106,7633,27704,524,3433,838,6699,27229,8161,11,18,15,1106,1502,20912,32,73315,32,49540,1215,2383,20912,33,73315,33,11,3741,7,15,5905,2383,20912,34,73315,34,22037,1215,2383,20912,35,73315,35,22037,54,1215,2383,20912,36,73315,36,21172,1215,2383,20912,37,73315,37,21172,1215,2383,20912,38,73315,38,11,3944,1215,2383,20912,39,73315,39,21172,317,24157,6405,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIn the figure below, isosceles $\\triangle ABC$ with base $\\overline{AB}$ has altitude $CH = 24$ cm. $DE = GF$, $HF = 12$ cm, and $FB = 6$ cm. What is the number of square centimeters in the area of pentagon $CDEFG$? [asy]\nimport olympiad; import geometry; size(200); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));\npair A = (-18,0), B = (18,0), C = (0,24), D = (-12,8), E = (-12,0), F = (12,0), G = (12,8), H = (0,0);\ndraw(A--C--B--cycle);\ndraw(D--E);\ndraw(G--F);\ndraw(C--H);\ndraw(rightanglemark(D,E,F,30));\ndraw(rightanglemark(C,H,F,30));\ndraw(rightanglemark(G,F,B,30));\nlabel(\"$A$\",A,W); label(\"$B$\",B,dir(0)); label(\"$C$\",C,N); label(\"$D$\",D,NW); label(\"$E$\",E,S); label(\"$F$\",F,S); label(\"$G$\",G,NE); label(\"$H$\",H,S);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"d04fc96f32a352f50d6c20ff22cbcb658a5fcbc392a4a4684e40fac441ce6475"},"seconds":40.30629515647888,"seed":372844770621886285,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to find the area of the pentagon $CDEFG$ in the given isosceles triangle $ABC$. We will follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the length of the base $AB$ of the triangle $ABC$.\n2. Calculate the area of the triangle $ABC$.\n3. Calculate the area of the rectangle $DEGF$.\n4. Subtract the area of the rectangle from the area of the triangle to find the area of the pentagon $CDEFG$.\n\nLet's start with step 1.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the length of the base $AB$\n\nSince $CH$ is the altitude of the isosceles triangle $ABC$ and $CH = 24$ cm, and $H$ is the midpoint of $AB$, we can use the Pythagorean theorem in $\\triangle ACH$ to find $AH$.\n\n\\[ AH = BF + HF = 6 + 12 = 18 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nSo, the length of the base $AB$ is:\n\n\\[ AB = 2 \\times AH = 2 \\times 18 = 36 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the area of the triangle $ABC$\n\nThe area of triangle $ABC$ is given by:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area of } \\triangle ABC = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 36 \\times 24 = 432 \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the area of the rectangle $DEGF$\n\nThe height of the rectangle $DEGF$ is the same as the height of the smaller triangle $CHF$, which is $CH - HF = 24 - 12 = 12$ cm. The width of the rectangle is the length of $DE$ or $GF$, which is the same as $AB - (AH - HF) - (BH - HF) = 36 - 18 - 18 + 2 \\times 12 = 24$ cm.\n\nSo, the area of the rectangle $DEGF$ is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area of } DEGF = \\text{width} \\times \\text{height} = 24 \\times 12 = 288 \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\n\n### Step 4: Calculate the area of the pentagon $CDEFG$\n\nThe area of the pentagon $CDEFG$ is the area of the triangle $ABC$ minus the area of the rectangle $DEGF$:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area of } CDEFG = 432 - 288 = 144 \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\n\nThus, the number of square centimeters in the area of pentagon $CDEFG$ is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{144}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{144}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
